export const LS1={title:'Getting ready: the event and the steps every skill shares',hook:'Life Support Skills teaches you to help someone in an emergency before an ambulance arrives. You will show a judge one CPR skill and one first aid skill. Many steps are the same in every skill. If you learn those shared steps well, every skill becomes easier.',objectives:['Explain how the Life Support Skills event works.','Pack the first aid kit you will bring.','Do the opening steps every skill shares: scene safety, gloves, checking the person and asking to help.','Call 911 correctly and finish every skill the right way.'],sections:[
['How the event works',[
'Life Support Skills is a **solo event**. That means you compete **by yourself**. It is for HOSA members who have an **IEP** under the **IDEA** law. Students with only a Section 504 plan cannot enter this event. You must send an **accommodation form** by **May 15**. If you are late, you can still compete, but you lose **35 points**.',
'There is **no written test**. You show the judge **one CPR skill** and **one first aid skill**. The judge reads you a short **scenario** (a story about the emergency). Then the timer starts. You need **70%** or more to win an award. Before you start, tell the judge if you follow the **American Heart Association (AHA)** or the **American Red Cross (ARC)**.',
{t:'table',caption:'The skills and time limits',head:['Type','Skill','Time'],rows:[['CPR','I. Adult CPR','6 minutes'],['CPR','II. Adult CPR with AED','8 minutes'],['CPR','III. Infant CPR','6 minutes'],['First aid','IV. EpiPen','3 minutes'],['First aid','V. Severe bleeding','5 minutes'],['First aid','VI. Choking','3 minutes'],['First aid','VII. Open fracture and splint','6 minutes'],['First aid','VIII. Heat emergency','5 minutes'],['First aid','IX. Severe burn','6 minutes'],['First aid','X. Naloxone','3 minutes']]}]],
['Your first aid kit',[
'You bring your own kit. It must have **2 cravats** (strips of cloth to tie a splint), **bandages** (sterile gauze squares, roller bandages, a trauma dressing and a non-stick dressing), **mouth-to-mask devices** for an **adult** and an **infant**, a **biohazard bag**, **hand sanitizer**, a **cell phone** to pretend to call 911, **bandage scissors**, **2 rolls of tape** and **4 pairs of non-latex gloves**. HOSA gives you a tourniquet, or you can bring your own.',
'Pack your kit the same way every time. Put gloves on top so you can grab them fast. Practice opening the kit and finding each item without looking for long. A neat kit saves time and helps you stay calm.',
sort('Is it in the kit?',['Bring it','Do not need it'],[['Non-latex gloves',0],['Adult and infant mouth-to-mask',0],['A blood pressure cuff',1],['Biohazard bag',0],['Cell phone for a pretend 911 call',0],['A thermometer',1]])]],
['Opening steps for every skill',[
'Most skills start the same way. **1. Check that the scene is safe.** Say it out loud: “The scene is safe.” **2. Open your kit and put on gloves.** **3. Check the person.** Tap them and shout, “Are you OK?” See if they are breathing. **4. If they are awake, say your name and ask, “May I help you?”** Wait for them to say yes. **5. Ask what happened.** **6. Look for medical jewelry**, like a bracelet or necklace that names a health problem.',
'Say each step out loud. The judge can only give points for what they **see** or **hear**. If you cannot do a step with real equipment, **say** what you would do. This is called **verbalizing**.',
order('Opening steps',['Say “The scene is safe”','Put on gloves','Tap and shout, check breathing','Say your name and ask “May I help you?”','Ask what happened','Look for medical jewelry'])]],
['Calling 911',[
'In most skills you must call **911** (EMS). Use your phone. Tell the dispatcher: **your name**, **where you are** and **your phone number**. Then say **what happened**, **how many people are hurt** and **how they are doing**. Do not hang up first. Wait until the dispatcher says it is OK. Then say to the judge, “The dispatcher said I can hang up.”',
'Some skills let you **tell a bystander** to call 911 instead. Point to them and say, “You, call 911 and come back.” Clear words help in a real emergency too.',
order('What to say on a 911 call',['My name is…','I am at… (location)','My phone number is…','What happened','How many people and how they are','Wait until the dispatcher says to hang up'])]],
['Finishing every skill',[
'When the judge says **“EMS has arrived,”** you finish. **Tell EMS (the judge) what happened** and what you did. Give times if you used a tourniquet or medicine. Then **take off your gloves** the safe way: pinch the outside of one glove at the wrist and peel it off, hold it in your other gloved hand, slide a bare finger under the second glove and peel it off over the first. Do not touch the outside with bare skin.',
'Put the gloves in the **biohazard bag**. Then clean your hands with **hand sanitizer**. Talk kindly to the person the whole time. These ending steps are worth points in every skill, so practice them every time.',
order('Taking off gloves',['Pinch the outside of one glove at the wrist','Peel it off and hold it in the other hand','Slide a bare finger under the second glove','Peel it off over the first glove','Put both in the biohazard bag','Use hand sanitizer'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
tip('Practice tip','Practice with a friend or teacher acting as the judge. Use a timer. Say every step out loud, even simple ones like “The scene is safe.”'),
kase('Start of a skill',{a:{text:'The judge reads: “You see a woman sitting on a bench holding her arm.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do first?',choices:[['Say “The scene is safe” and put on gloves','b','Correct. Safety and gloves come first.',true],['Run and grab her arm','b','Check safety and put on gloves first.',false]]},b:{text:'She is awake. What do you say?',choices:[['“My name is ___. May I help you?”','c','Correct. Ask permission.',true],['Nothing; just start helping','c','Always ask first.',false]]},c:{text:'She says yes. You ask what happened and look for medical jewelry.',end:true}})]],
["Staying calm and practicing well",["It is normal to feel nervous. Take a slow, deep breath before the judge reads the scenario. Listen to the whole story. If you did not hear something, you can ask the judge to repeat it. You may also ask the judge questions about the patient, like “Is the person breathing?” or “Is the bleeding stopping?”","Practice the same way every time. Use a checklist made from the rating sheet. Have a teacher or friend read a scenario and time you. After each practice, look at the checklist and see which steps you missed. Then practice those steps again. Short practices on many days work better than one long practice.","Use your **accommodations**. Your accommodation form tells HOSA what help you need, like extra reading support. Talk with your advisor early so the form is sent on time. Bring your photo ID and wear clothes that are right for a health care job.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"A good practice routine","steps":["Read the rating sheet steps","Have someone read you a scenario","Do the skill with a timer","Check the steps you missed","Practice the missed steps","Repeat on another day"]}]]
],takeaways:['Solo event for students with an IEP under IDEA; send the accommodation form by May 15 or lose 35 points; no written test; one CPR skill and one first aid skill; need 70%; say AHA or ARC first.','Opening steps: say the scene is safe, put on gloves, tap and shout and check breathing, say your name and ask “May I help you?”, ask what happened, look for medical jewelry.','911 call: your name, location, phone number, what happened, how many and how they are; hang up only when told; finish by telling EMS, removing gloves safely, biohazard bag and hand sanitizer.'],terms:[['EMS','Emergency medical services—the ambulance team you reach by calling 911.'],['Verbalize','Say out loud what you are doing or would do.'],['PPE','Personal protective equipment, like gloves.']],mcqs:[q('How many people compete together in Life Support Skills?','One (solo)','Two','Three','Four','You compete alone.'),q('What is the first step in every skill?','Make sure the scene is safe','Call your parents','Give medicine','Take off your gloves','Safety first.'),q('What do you ask an awake person before helping?','“May I help you?”','“How old are you?”','“Where do you live?”','Nothing','Ask permission.'),q('When can you hang up on a 911 call?','When the dispatcher says it is OK','Right away','After 5 seconds','Never','Wait to be told.'),q('Where do used gloves go?','In the biohazard bag','In your pocket','On the floor','Back in the kit','Then use sanitizer.'),q('What score do you need to win an award?','70% or higher','50%','100%','30%','On your skills.'),q('What is the last step of every skill?','Clean your hands with hand sanitizer','Put on new gloves','Call 911 again','Leave the room','Hand hygiene.'),q('When must the accommodation form be sent?','By May 15','By December 1','After the event','It is not needed','Or lose 35 points.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the opening steps and the ending steps that you do in almost every Life Support Skills skill, including how to call 911.',rubric:[['Opening steps',['scene','safe','gloves','tap','shout','breathing','name','may i help','what happened','jewelry']],['911 call',['911','name','location','phone','what happened','how many','hang up','dispatcher']],['Ending steps',['ems','tell','gloves','outside','biohazard','hand sanitizer']],['Communication',['out loud','verbalize','calm','kind','judge']]],model:'At the start of a skill, I say out loud, “The scene is safe,” then open my first aid kit and put on gloves. I check the person by tapping them and shouting, “Are you OK?” and I look to see if they are breathing. If they are awake, I say, “My name is ___. May I help you?” and wait for them to say yes. Then I ask what happened and look for medical jewelry, like a bracelet that names a health problem. When I call 911, I tell the dispatcher my name, where I am and my phone number, then what happened, how many people are hurt and how they are doing. I do not hang up until the dispatcher says it is OK, and I tell the judge that the dispatcher said I could hang up. When the judge says EMS has arrived, I tell EMS what happened and what I did, including any times. I take off my gloves without touching the outside with my bare hands, put them in the biohazard bag and clean my hands with hand sanitizer. I say every step out loud so the judge can hear it, and I talk calmly and kindly to the person the whole time.'},sources:[RULES,ARC_STEPS,AHA_CPR]};

export const LS2={title:'Adult CPR (Skill I)',hook:'When a person’s heart stops, they fall down, stop breathing normally and have no pulse. CPR pushes blood to the brain until help comes. You do not have to be a doctor to do CPR. Skill I shows the judge how you do CPR by yourself on an adult when no AED is available.',objectives:['Know when a person needs CPR.','Call for help and check breathing and pulse.','Give 30 strong chest compressions and 2 breaths.','Keep going for 5 cycles until EMS arrives, following HOSA Skill I.'],sections:[
['When someone needs CPR',[
'A person needs CPR when they are **not responding**, **not breathing normally** and have **no pulse**. Gasping is **not** normal breathing. Their heart has stopped pumping blood. Without blood, the brain starts to get hurt in a few minutes. CPR pushes blood around the body. It keeps the person alive until EMS and a defibrillator arrive.',
'In Skill I, you have **6 minutes**. The judge says **no AED is available**. You must call 911 yourself. Then you do CPR by yourself for about **2 minutes (5 cycles)** until the judge says EMS has arrived.',
tip('Remember','Not responding + not breathing normally + no pulse = start CPR.')]],
['Steps 1–6: check and call',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe and tell the judge **AHA or ARC**. **2.** Open your kit and put on gloves. **3.** **Tap or gently shake the shoulder** and shout, **“Are you OK?”** The judge says the person is not responding. **4.** **Shout for nearby help**: “Someone help! Call 911!” The judge says an AED is not available. **5.** **Call 911** yourself: your name, location and phone number; what happened, how many people and how they are; hang up only when the dispatcher says so.',
'**6.** Check **breathing and pulse** for **at least 5 seconds but no more than 10 seconds**. **AHA:** look at the chest to see if it goes up and down. **ARC:** tilt the head back and lift the chin, then check breathing and pulse at the same time. Feel the pulse on the **neck**: slide **2 or 3 fingers** into the groove next to the windpipe. The judge says the person is not breathing and has no pulse.',
order('Steps 1–6',['Say “The scene is safe” and AHA or ARC','Put on gloves','Tap the shoulder and shout “Are you OK?”','Shout for help','Call 911 and give your information','Check breathing and neck pulse for 5–10 seconds'])]],
['Step 7: chest compressions',[
'Kneel **next to** the person. Move bulky clothes out of the way. Make sure they are **face up on a firm, flat surface** like the floor. Put the **heel of one hand** in the **center of the chest**, on the **lower half of the breastbone**. Put your **other hand on top**. Keep your **arms straight** and your **shoulders right over your hands**.',
'Push **hard and fast**: at least **2 inches** deep, at a speed of **100 to 120 per minute**. That means **30 pushes in 15 to 18 seconds**. **Count out loud**: “1, 2, 3…” After each push, let the chest **come all the way back up**. Do not lean on it. Many people use the beat of a song with about 100–120 beats per minute to keep the speed.',
"Pushing hard on the chest is tiring. That is normal. Use your body weight, not just your arms. Bend at your hips, not your elbows.",
flip('Compression numbers',[['How deep','At least 2 inches'],['How fast','100–120 per minute'],['How many','30 pushes'],['How long for 30','15 to 18 seconds'],['Where','Center of the chest, lower half of breastbone'],['After each push','Let the chest come back up']])]],
['Steps 8–9: breaths with a mask',[
'**8.** Put the **mouth-to-mask** on the face. Use the **bridge of the nose** to line it up. **9.** Seal the mask: put your **thumb and pointer finger** of the hand closest to the top of the head along the **top** of the mask. Put the **thumb** of your other hand along the **bottom** of the mask. Put your other fingers under the **jaw bone** and **lift**. This tilts the head back and opens the airway.',
'Press firmly all around the edge so no air leaks out. Give **2 breaths**. Each breath lasts **1 second**. Watch the chest **rise**. If the chest does not rise, tilt the head again and make a better seal. Do not blow too hard.',
order('Mask breaths',['Place the mask using the bridge of the nose','Thumb and finger on the top edge','Other thumb on the bottom edge','Fingers lift the jaw','Press all around to seal','Give 2 breaths, 1 second each'])]],
['Steps 10–15: keep going and finish',[
'**10.** Keep doing **30 pushes and 2 breaths**. Do **5 cycles**, about **2 minutes**. Stop for **less than 5 seconds** each time you give breaths. The judge says EMS has arrived and taken over.',
'**11.** Talk clearly to others. **12.** Tell EMS what happened and what you did. **13.** Take off your gloves without touching the outside. **14.** Put them in the biohazard bag. **15.** Use hand sanitizer. Skill I is worth **64 points**. You need **44.8** to reach 70%.',
order('Skill I order',['Scene safe, AHA or ARC, gloves','Tap and shout','Shout for help and call 911','Check breathing and pulse','30 compressions','2 breaths with the mask','5 cycles until EMS arrives','Report, gloves, biohazard bag, sanitizer'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Man collapses at the store',{a:{text:'A man falls down in a store. He does not answer when you tap him. (Scenario invented for practice.) What next?',choices:[['Shout for help and call 911','b','Correct.',true],['Walk away to find a manager','b','Stay and call for help.',false]]},b:{text:'He is not breathing and has no pulse. Where do you put your hands?',choices:[['Center of the chest, lower half of the breastbone','c','Correct.',true],['On his stomach','c','Hands go on the chest.',false]]},c:{text:'You give 30 compressions and 2 breaths, five times, until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Common CPR mistakes",["Many people push **too softly** because they are afraid of hurting the person. A person whose heart has stopped needs **hard** pushes. Push at least 2 inches. Another mistake is pushing **too slowly** or **too fast**. Practice with a song or a metronome to find the right speed.","Some people **lean** on the chest and do not let it come back up. The heart fills with blood when the chest comes up, so let it rise all the way. Others stop for too long to give breaths. Keep pauses **short**—less than 5 seconds.","Keep your **arms straight** and your **shoulders over your hands**. Bent arms get tired fast and do not push deep enough. If you start to feel tired, keep going until help comes. In the event, you only do about 2 minutes, but real CPR can last longer.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Good CPR or a mistake?","buckets":["Good CPR","Mistake"],"items":[["Pushing at least 2 inches deep",0],["Leaning on the chest between pushes",1],["Arms straight, shoulders over hands",0],["Stopping for 20 seconds between cycles",1],["Counting out loud",0],["Pushing very softly",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['CPR is needed when a person is not responding, not breathing normally (gasping is not breathing) and has no pulse; Skill I is 6 minutes, alone, with no AED.','Tap or gently shake the shoulder and shout, shout for help, call 911 yourself, check breathing and the neck pulse for 5–10 seconds.','30 compressions (at least 2 inches, 100–120 per minute, 15–18 seconds, count out loud, let the chest come up), 2 mask breaths of 1 second each, 5 cycles until EMS arrives, then report, gloves, biohazard bag and sanitizer.'],terms:[['CPR','Cardiopulmonary resuscitation—chest pushes and breaths when the heart stops.'],['Compression','A hard, fast push on the chest.'],['Cycle','30 compressions and 2 breaths.']],mcqs:[q('How deep do you push on an adult’s chest?','At least 2 inches','Half an inch','6 inches','1 centimeter','Push hard.'),q('How many compressions come before the 2 breaths?','30','15','5','100','30:2.'),q('How fast do you push?','100–120 per minute','20 per minute','200 per minute','10 per minute','Fast.'),q('Where do you feel for the pulse on an adult?','On the neck, next to the windpipe','On the wrist only','On the foot','On the ear','Carotid pulse.'),q('How long do you check for breathing and pulse?','5 to 10 seconds','1 minute','1 second','30 seconds','Not longer than 10.'),q('How long is each breath?','1 second','5 seconds','10 seconds','Half a minute','Watch the chest rise.'),q('How many cycles do you do before EMS arrives in Skill I?','5 (about 2 minutes)','1','20','100','About 2 minutes.'),q('Is gasping normal breathing?','No','Yes','Only in adults','Only at night','Start CPR.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you do adult CPR by yourself following HOSA Skill I, from checking the person to when EMS arrives.',rubric:[['Start',['scene','safe','aha','arc','gloves','tap','shout','are you ok']],['Help and check',['shout for help','911','name','location','breathing','pulse','neck','10 seconds']],['Compressions',['side','firm','flat','heel','center','breastbone','arms straight','2 inches','100','120','30','count','come back up']],['Breaths and finish',['mask','bridge of the nose','seal','lift','2 breaths','1 second','5 cycles','ems','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'First I say, “The scene is safe,” and tell the judge whether I follow AHA or ARC. I put on gloves, tap the man’s shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?” He does not answer, so I shout for help and call 911 myself, giving my name, location and phone number and saying what happened, how many people are hurt and how they are, and I wait for the dispatcher to say I can hang up. I check for breathing and a pulse on the neck, using two or three fingers next to the windpipe, for 5 to 10 seconds. He is not breathing and has no pulse, so I kneel next to him on the firm, flat floor and move his jacket away. I put the heel of one hand on the center of his chest, on the lower half of the breastbone, and the other hand on top. With straight arms and my shoulders over my hands, I push at least 2 inches deep, 30 times in 15 to 18 seconds, counting out loud and letting the chest come all the way back up. Then I place the mask using the bridge of the nose, seal it with my thumbs and fingers, lift the jaw and give 2 breaths of 1 second each while watching the chest rise. I do 5 cycles of 30 compressions and 2 breaths until EMS arrives. Then I tell EMS what happened, take off my gloves safely, put them in the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,ARC_CPR,MEDLINE_CPR]};

export const LS3={title:'Adult CPR with an AED (Skill II)',hook:'An AED is a small machine that can shock a stopped heart back into a normal rhythm. It talks to you and tells you what to do. Anyone can use one. Skill II is like Skill I, but an AED arrives while you are doing CPR, and you have 8 minutes.',objectives:['Explain what an AED does.','Start CPR the same way as Skill I.','Turn on the AED, place the pads and follow its voice.','Give a shock safely and start CPR again right away, following HOSA Skill II.'],sections:[
['What an AED does',[
'**AED** stands for **automated external defibrillator**. When the heart stops, it often **quivers** instead of pumping. The AED **checks the heart rhythm**. If a shock can help, it tells you to give one. The shock can help the heart start beating normally again. The AED will **not** shock a person who does not need it.',
"You cannot hurt someone by using an AED on them, because it only shocks when it is needed. That is why anyone can use one, even without training.",
'AEDs are in schools, gyms, malls and airports. They are often in a box on the wall with a heart and lightning bolt sign. Using an AED **fast** saves lives. CPR keeps blood moving until the AED is ready.',
"When you hear an AED talk, listen carefully. It may say things like “Apply pads to patient’s bare chest,” “Analyzing heart rhythm, do not touch the patient” or “Shock advised.” Some AEDs give the shock by themselves after a countdown. Others need you to press a flashing button. Either way, make sure no one is touching the person.",
tip('Remember','The AED talks to you. Turn it on first and do what it says.')]],
['Steps 1–10: start CPR',[
'Skill II starts like Skill I. **1.** Scene safe and AHA or ARC. **2.** Gloves. **3.** Tap or gently shake the shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?” **4.** Shout for help: “Call 911 and **get an AED**!” The judge says an AED is available. **5.** Call 911 with your name, location, phone number, what happened and how many people; hang up when told. **6.** Check breathing and the neck pulse for 5–10 seconds.',
'**7.** Give **30 compressions**: center of the chest, at least 2 inches, 100–120 per minute, count out loud, let the chest come back up. **8–9.** Give **2 breaths** with the mask. **10.** Keep doing **30 and 2**, with pauses of less than 5 seconds. Then the judge says, “The AED has arrived,” and hands you the AED.',
order('Skill II opening',['Scene safe; AHA or ARC; gloves','Tap and shout','Shout for help and an AED','Call 911','Check breathing and pulse','Start 30 compressions and 2 breaths'])]],
['Steps 11–15: use the AED',[
'**11.** Open the case and **turn on the AED**. **12.** Put the **pads** on the person’s **bare chest**. Look at the pictures on the pads. One usually goes on the **upper right chest**, the other on the **lower left side**. Do **not** put pads over clothes, **medicine patches** or a **bump under the skin** (an implanted device). **13.** **Clear** the person: make sure no one is touching them. Let the AED **analyze** (check the heart).',
'**14.** If the AED says to shock, **look** around and say loudly, **“All clear!”** Then press the **shock** button. **15.** Right after the shock—or if the AED says no shock—**start CPR again** with **chest compressions**. Do not wait. The judge then says EMS has arrived.',
order('Using the AED',['Open the case and turn it on','Put pads on the bare chest','Make sure no one touches the person','Let the AED analyze','Look and say “All clear!” then shock','Start compressions again right away'])]],
['AED safety',[
'Never touch the person while the AED is checking or shocking. If the person is in **water**, move them out and **dry the chest** before using the pads. If you see a **medicine patch** on the chest, take it off with your gloved hand and wipe the skin. If there is a **bump** under the skin (a pacemaker), put the pad a little to the side.',
'If the chest is very hairy and the pads will not stick, press them down hard. Some kits have a razor to shave the spot. For a **child**, use **child pads** if you have them. If you only have adult pads, you can use them, but they must **not touch** each other.',
match('AED problem and what to do',[['Wet chest','Dry it before putting on pads'],['Medicine patch','Take it off and wipe the skin'],['Bump under the skin','Put the pad to the side'],['Hairy chest','Press pads hard or shave'],['Child','Use child pads if you have them'],['During the shock','No one touches the person']])]],
['Steps 16–20: finish',[
'When EMS arrives: **16.** Talk clearly. **17.** Tell EMS what happened, what you did and how many shocks the AED gave. **18.** Take off your gloves safely. **19.** Put them in the biohazard bag. **20.** Use hand sanitizer.',
'Skill II is worth **74 points**. You need **51.8** to reach 70%. You have **8 minutes**, which is more than Skill I because of the AED steps. Practice turning on a training AED and placing pads until it feels easy.',
chk('What do you do right after the AED gives a shock?',['Start chest compressions again','Check the pulse for a minute','Take off the pads','Wait for EMS without doing anything'],'Start CPR again right away, beginning with compressions.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('AED at the gym',{a:{text:'You are doing CPR on a woman at a gym. Someone brings an AED. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do first with the AED?',choices:[['Turn it on','b','Correct. Then follow its voice.',true],['Read the whole manual','b','Turn it on and follow the prompts.',false]]},b:{text:'The pads are on. The AED says, “Shock advised.” What next?',choices:[['Look, say “All clear!” and press shock','c','Correct.',true],['Hold her hand while it shocks','c','Never touch during a shock.',false]]},c:{text:'You start compressions right after the shock and keep going until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Common AED mistakes",["The biggest mistake is **waiting**. Start CPR right away and use the AED as soon as it arrives. Do not stop CPR for a long time while you open the case. Turn the AED on first—it will tell you what to do next.","Another mistake is putting the pads on **over clothes** or on the wrong spots. Open or cut the shirt so the chest is bare. Look at the pictures on the pads. Press the pads down firmly so they stick.","Never touch the person while the AED is checking the heart or giving a shock. Always look and say, “All clear!” After the shock, do not stop to check the pulse. Start pushing on the chest **right away**. The AED will tell you when it will check again.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Good AED use or a mistake?","buckets":["Good","Mistake"],"items":[["Turning the AED on first",0],["Putting pads over a shirt",1],["Saying “All clear!” before a shock",0],["Holding the person’s hand during the shock",1],["Starting compressions right after the shock",0],["Waiting a long time before using the AED",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['An AED checks the heart rhythm and tells you if a shock is needed; Skill II is 8 minutes and starts like Skill I, but you shout for help and an AED.','Turn on the AED, put pads on the bare chest (not on clothes, medicine patches or implanted devices), make sure no one is touching, let it analyze, say “All clear!” and shock if advised.','Start compressions right after the shock or a no-shock message; dry a wet chest; child pads for children; finish by reporting to EMS, removing gloves, biohazard bag and sanitizer.'],terms:[['AED','Automated external defibrillator—a machine that can shock the heart.'],['Pads','Sticky pads that connect the AED to the chest.'],['Analyze','When the AED checks the heart’s rhythm.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first thing you do with the AED?','Turn it on','Put it away','Shake it','Plug it into the wall','Then follow its voice.'),q('Where do the AED pads go?','On the bare chest','Over a shirt','On the arms','On the back of the head','Follow the pictures.'),q('What do you say before pressing the shock button?','“All clear!”','“Good luck!”','“Ready, set, go!”','Nothing','Make sure no one is touching.'),q('What do you do right after a shock?','Start compressions again','Stop CPR','Remove the pads','Give water','Do not wait.'),q('What should you do if the chest is wet?','Dry it first','Use the AED anyway','Pour more water','Skip the AED','Dry before pads.'),q('How long do you have for Skill II?','8 minutes','3 minutes','20 minutes','1 minute','More time for the AED.'),q('Can anyone touch the person while the AED analyzes?','No','Yes','Only family','Only the judge','Stay clear.'),q('What do you do with a medicine patch on the chest?','Take it off and wipe the skin','Put the pad on top','Leave it and skip the AED','Add another patch','Gloved hand.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you use an AED during adult CPR following HOSA Skill II, including safety steps.',rubric:[['Start of CPR',['scene','gloves','tap','shout','aed','911','breathing','pulse','30','2 breaths']],['AED steps',['turn on','pads','bare chest','pictures','analyze','no one touching']],['Shock and after',['all clear','shock','start compressions','right away','no shock']],['Safety and finish',['water','dry','patch','bump','child pads','ems','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'I start like Skill I: I say the scene is safe, tell the judge AHA or ARC, put on gloves, tap the shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?” and then shout for someone to call 911 and get an AED. I call 911 myself with my name, location, phone number and what happened, check breathing and the neck pulse for 5–10 seconds, and start 30 compressions and 2 breaths. When the AED arrives, I open the case and turn it on first, because it tells me what to do. I put the pads on the bare chest using the pictures, usually upper right chest and lower left side, not over clothes, medicine patches or a bump from an implanted device. I make sure no one is touching the person while the AED analyzes the heart. If it says to shock, I look around, say loudly, “All clear!” and press the shock button. Right after the shock, or if it says no shock, I start chest compressions again without waiting. To stay safe, I dry the chest if it is wet, take off any medicine patch and wipe the skin, put the pad to the side of a pacemaker and use child pads for a child if I have them. When EMS arrives, I tell them what happened and how many shocks were given, take off my gloves safely, put them in the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,ARC_AED,AHA_CPR]};

export const LS4={title:'Infant CPR (Skill III)',hook:'An infant is a baby younger than 1 year old. Babies are small, so CPR is gentler. You use fingers or thumbs, smaller breaths and a baby-size mask. In Skill III you are alone, so you do CPR for 2 minutes before you call 911.',objectives:['Explain how infant CPR is different from adult CPR.','Check an infant for breathing and a pulse on the upper arm.','Give 30 gentle but strong compressions and 2 small breaths.','Do 2 minutes of CPR, call 911, then keep going, following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['How infant CPR is different',[
'An **infant** is a baby **under 1 year old**. Babies often stop breathing because of a breathing problem, like choking or drowning. That is why giving **breaths** is very important. In Skill III, you are **alone** with **no AED**. You do **2 minutes of CPR first**, then call 911. You have **6 minutes**.',
'Everything is smaller for a baby. You check the pulse on the **upper arm**, not the neck. You push with **one hand** or **two thumbs**. You push about **1½ inches** deep. You give **small breaths** with an **infant mask**. You keep the baby’s head **straight (neutral)**, not tilted far back.',
"For a **child** (from age 1 to about puberty), CPR is in between. You can use one or two hands and push about 2 inches. In the event, Skill III is always an infant. Still, learning the differences helps you understand why each step matters.",
{t:'table',caption:'Adult and infant CPR',head:['','Adult','Infant'],rows:[['Pulse check','Neck','Upper arm'],['Hands','Two hands','Heel of one hand or two thumbs'],['Depth','At least 2 inches','About 1½ inches (one-third of the chest)'],['Head','Tilt back','Straight (neutral)'],['When to call 911 (alone)','Right away','After 2 minutes of CPR'],['Ratio','30:2','30:2']]}]],
['Steps 1–5: check the baby',[
'**1.** Say the scene is safe and AHA or ARC. **2.** Gloves. **3.** **Tap or gently shake** the shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?” Never shake a baby hard. The judge says the baby is not responding. **4.** **Shout for help**: “Someone call 911 and get an AED!” The judge says no AED is available.',
'**5.** Check breathing and pulse for **5 to 10 seconds**. **AHA:** look at the chest. **ARC:** put the head in a **straight (neutral)** position without pressing on the soft neck. Feel the **brachial pulse**: put **2 or 3 fingers** on the **inside of the upper arm**, halfway between the elbow and shoulder. The judge says the baby is not breathing and has no pulse.',
order('Steps 1–5',['Scene safe; AHA or ARC','Gloves','Tap or gently shake the shoulder and shout','Shout for help','Check breathing and upper-arm pulse for 5–10 seconds'])]],
['Step 6: infant compressions',[
'Put the baby **face up on a firm, flat surface**, like a table. Stand or kneel at the baby’s side. Find the **center of the chest just below the nipple line**. Use the **heel of one hand**, or **wrap both hands around the chest** and push with **two thumbs**.',
'Push about **one-third of the chest depth—about 1½ inches (4 cm)**. Go **100 to 120 per minute**: **30 pushes in 15 to 18 seconds**. **Count out loud**. Let the chest **come back up** after each push. Babies are small, but you still need to push firmly enough to move blood.',
"With two thumbs, wrap your hands around the baby’s chest and press with both thumbs side by side. With one hand, use only the heel of your hand. Choose the way that works best for you and practice it.",
flip('Infant compressions',[['Where','Center of the chest, just below the nipple line'],['With what','Heel of one hand or two thumbs'],['How deep','About 1½ inches'],['How fast','100–120 per minute'],['How many','30'],['After each push','Let the chest come back up']])]],
['Steps 7–10: breaths, call and continue',[
'**7.** Put the **infant mask** on the face, using the **bridge of the nose**. Keep the head **straight (neutral)**. Give **2 small breaths**, **1 second** each. Just enough to see the chest **rise**. **8.** Keep doing **30 pushes and 2 breaths** for **2 minutes**. Keep pauses short—less than 5 seconds.',
'**9.** After 2 minutes, **call 911**: your name, location and phone number; what happened, how many people and how they are; hang up only when told. **10.** Go right back to **30 and 2** until the judge says EMS has arrived. **11–15.** Talk clearly, tell EMS what happened, take off gloves safely, use the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.',
order('Skill III order',['Scene safe, AHA or ARC, gloves','Tap and shout','Shout for help','Check breathing and upper-arm pulse','30 compressions just below the nipple line','2 small breaths','2 minutes of CPR','Call 911, then continue until EMS'])]],
['Keeping babies safe',[
'Many baby emergencies can be prevented. Put babies to sleep **alone**, **on their back**, in a **crib** with no pillows, blankets or toys. Never leave a baby alone in a bathtub or near water. Keep small objects like coins, button batteries and balloons away from babies. Cut food into very small pieces.',
'Learn infant CPR with a teacher and a baby manikin. Practice finding the nipple line and the upper-arm pulse. In Skill III, judges watch for gentle but firm pushes, small breaths and calling 911 **after 2 minutes**.',
sort('Safe or unsafe for a baby?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Sleeping alone on the back in a crib',0],['Sleeping with pillows and stuffed toys',1],['Leaving a baby alone in the bathtub',1],['Keeping coins out of reach',0],['Cutting food into tiny pieces',0],['Letting a baby play with a balloon',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Baby not breathing',{a:{text:'You find a baby who does not move when you tap and shout. You are alone. (Scenario invented for practice.) Where do you check the pulse?',choices:[['Inside of the upper arm','b','Correct. The brachial pulse.',true],['On the neck','b','For babies, use the upper arm.',false]]},b:{text:'There is no pulse. Where do you push?',choices:[['Center of the chest just below the nipple line','c','Correct.',true],['On the stomach','c','Push on the chest.',false]]},c:{text:'You do 2 minutes of CPR, then call 911, then keep going until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Common infant CPR mistakes",["Some people push **too gently** on a baby because the baby is small. You still need to push about **1½ inches**—one-third of the chest. Others push in the wrong spot. Push in the **center** of the chest, **just below the nipple line**, not on the belly.","Do not tilt a baby’s head **too far back**. This can close the baby’s soft airway. Keep the head **straight**. Do not give **big breaths**. Give small breaths, just enough to make the chest rise.","Remember the order when you are **alone**: do **2 minutes of CPR first**, then call 911. Use the **infant mask**, not the adult mask. A mask that is too big will not seal and will cover the eyes.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Good infant CPR or a mistake?","buckets":["Good","Mistake"],"items":[["Head straight (neutral)",0],["Head tilted far back",1],["Small breaths that make the chest rise",0],["Pushing on the belly",1],["Using the infant mask",0],["Calling 911 after 2 minutes of CPR when alone",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Infant = under 1 year; alone with no AED, do 2 minutes of CPR before calling 911; Skill III is 6 minutes.','Tap or gently shake the shoulder and shout (never shake hard), shout for help, check breathing and the upper-arm (brachial) pulse for 5–10 seconds, head straight (neutral).','30 compressions just below the nipple line with the heel of one hand or two thumbs, about 1½ inches, 100–120 per minute; 2 small breaths of 1 second with an infant mask; after 2 minutes call 911 and continue.'],terms:[['Infant','A baby younger than 1 year.'],['Brachial pulse','The pulse on the inside of a baby’s upper arm.'],['Neutral position','Keeping a baby’s head straight, not tilted far back.']],mcqs:[q('Where do you check an infant’s pulse?','Inside of the upper arm','On the neck','On the ankle','On the forehead','Brachial pulse.'),q('Where do you push for infant CPR?','Center of the chest just below the nipple line','On the stomach','On the back','On the neck','Lower half of the breastbone.'),q('How deep do you push on an infant?','About 1½ inches','At least 3 inches','A tiny tap','4 inches','One-third of the chest.'),q('In Skill III, when do you call 911?','After 2 minutes of CPR','Before checking the baby','Never','After 20 minutes','You are alone.'),q('How should the baby’s head be for breaths?','Straight (neutral)','Tilted far back','Turned to the side','Bent forward','Neutral.'),q('How many compressions before breaths?','30','5','10','50','30:2 alone.'),q('Which mask do you use for a baby?','Infant mask','Adult mask','No mask','A paper bag','Correct size.'),q('Should you ever shake a baby hard?','No','Yes','Only if crying','Only at night','Tap or gently shake only.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how infant CPR is different from adult CPR and describe the steps of HOSA Skill III.',rubric:[['Differences',['under 1','upper arm','brachial','one hand','two thumbs','1½ inches','neutral','2 minutes']],['Checking',['scene','gloves','tap','shout','help','breathing','pulse','5','10 seconds']],['Compressions and breaths',['firm','flat','nipple line','100','120','30','count','come back up','infant mask','small breaths','1 second']],['Calling and finishing',['2 minutes','911','name','location','continue','ems','gloves','biohazard','sanitizer']]],model:'Infant CPR is for babies under 1 year old. Unlike adult CPR, I feel the pulse on the inside of the upper arm instead of the neck, push with the heel of one hand or two thumbs instead of two hands, push about 1½ inches deep instead of at least 2 inches, keep the head straight instead of tilting it far back and give small breaths with an infant mask. Because I am alone and babies usually stop breathing first, I do 2 minutes of CPR before calling 911. To start, I say the scene is safe, tell the judge AHA or ARC and put on gloves. I tap or gently shake the baby’s shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?” and then shout for someone to call 911 and get an AED. I check for breathing and the upper-arm pulse for 5 to 10 seconds. The baby is not breathing and has no pulse, so I place the baby face up on a firm, flat surface and push in the center of the chest just below the nipple line, 30 times in 15 to 18 seconds, about 1½ inches deep, counting out loud and letting the chest come back up. Then I put the infant mask on using the bridge of the nose, keep the head straight and give 2 small breaths of 1 second each. After 2 minutes of 30 and 2, I call 911 with my name, location and phone number and what happened, and then I keep doing CPR until EMS arrives. Finally, I tell EMS what happened, take off my gloves safely, use the biohazard bag and use hand sanitizer.'},sources:[RULES,ARC_CHILD,AHA_CPR]};

export const LS5={title:'Choking (Skill VI)',hook:'Food or a small object can get stuck in the throat and block air. A person who is choking badly cannot talk, cough or breathe. Back blows and belly pushes can get the object out. Skill VI is short—only 3 minutes—so you must know the steps well.',objectives:['Tell the difference between mild and severe choking.','Ask permission to help a choking person.','Give 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts, following HOSA Skill VI.','Know what to do after the object comes out.'],sections:[
['Mild or severe choking?',[
'With **mild choking**, the person can still **cough hard**, **talk** or **breathe**. Let them keep coughing. Stay with them. Coughing is the best way to get the object out.',
"Do not hit the back of a person who is coughing well. Do not give them water. Do not put your fingers in their mouth to search for the object. Just stay close, keep them calm and be ready to help if the coughing stops working.",
'With **severe choking**, the person **cannot talk, cough or breathe**. They may hold their **throat** with their hands. This is the **choking sign**. Their face may turn red, then blue. They need help **right now**. In Skill VI, you must **say out loud** the signs you see, like “She cannot talk or cough and is holding her throat.”',
sort('Mild or severe?',['Mild—let them cough','Severe—help now'],[['Coughing hard and talking',0],['Cannot talk or cough',1],['Hands on the throat with no sound',1],['Can say “I’m OK” between coughs',0],['Lips turning blue',1]])]],
['Steps 1–2: ask to help',[
'**1.** Look at the person and say the signs of **severe** choking out loud. **2.** Ask, **“Are you choking? Can I help you?”** The person will **nod yes** but cannot talk. Nodding means they agree to your help.',
"If the person shakes their head no, do not touch them, but stay close and call 911. A person who is choking badly may soon pass out. If that happens, you can help them, because a person who cannot answer is assumed to want help.",
'Stay calm. Speak clearly. Tell the person what you are going to do: “I am going to hit your back and push on your belly to get it out.” Skill VI does not need gloves or a 911 call in the rating sheet, but in a real emergency, have someone call 911 if the person does not get better fast.',
order('Steps 1–2',['See that the person cannot talk, cough or breathe','Say the signs out loud','Ask “Are you choking?”','Ask “Can I help you?”','The person nods yes'])]],
['Step 3: back blows',[
'**a.** Stand **to the side** and **a little behind** the person. **b.** Put **one arm across their chest** to hold them up. **c.** **Bend them forward** at the waist so their upper body is almost **flat, like a table**. **d.** With the **heel of your hand**, give **5 firm back blows** **between the shoulder blades**.',
'Each back blow is a **separate** hit to knock the object loose. Count them out loud: “1, 2, 3, 4, 5.” In the event, you **pretend** (simulate) the back blows, so do not really hit the person-actor hard.',
"Keep holding the person with your arm across their chest so they do not fall forward. Bending them forward helps gravity pull the object out of the throat. If the object comes out during the back blows, you do not need to give the belly pushes.",
order('Back blows',['Stand to the side and a little behind','Put one arm across the chest','Bend the person forward','Use the heel of your hand','Give 5 back blows between the shoulder blades'])]],
['Steps 4–7: abdominal thrusts',[
'**4.** Stand or kneel **behind** the person and **wrap your arms** around their **waist**. **5.** Make a **fist** with one hand. **6.** Put the **thumb side** of your fist on the belly, in the **middle**, **just above the belly button** and **well below the breastbone**. Hold your fist with your other hand.',
'Push **in and up** quickly. This is an **abdominal thrust**. **7.** **Switch back and forth**: **5 back blows**, then **5 abdominal thrusts**, until the object comes out. In the event, you **pretend** the thrusts. The judge says, “The object is expelled.”',
"Each abdominal thrust should be a quick, separate push. Keep your fist below the breastbone so you do not hurt the ribs. Kneel down if the person is shorter than you, like a child. Keep counting out loud so the judge can follow you.",
flip('Abdominal thrusts',[['Where you stand','Behind the person'],['Your hand','A fist, thumb side in'],['Where','Middle of the belly, just above the belly button'],['Not on','The breastbone'],['Direction','In and up'],['Pattern','5 back blows, then 5 thrusts']])]],
['Steps 8–10: after the object comes out',[
'**8.** Tell the person to **see a doctor right away**. Abdominal thrusts can hurt the inside of the body, so a doctor should check them. **9.** Talk kindly and clearly. **10.** Use **hand sanitizer**. Skill VI is worth **22 points**. You need **15.4** to reach 70%.',
'If the person **becomes unresponsive**, help them to the ground, call 911 and start **CPR**. Before giving breaths, look in the mouth. Only take an object out if you can **see** it. For a **pregnant** or **very large** person, push on the **chest** instead of the belly.',
chk('What do you tell the person after the object comes out?',['See a doctor right away','Go back to eating','Drink a lot of water quickly','Nothing'],'Abdominal thrusts can cause injury, so they need a check.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choking at lunch',{a:{text:'A classmate is holding their throat and cannot talk. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you say?',choices:[['“Are you choking? Can I help you?”','b','Correct.',true],['“Stop being silly.”','b','Severe choking is an emergency.',false]]},b:{text:'They nod yes. What do you do first?',choices:[['Stand to the side and a little behind, bend them forward and give 5 back blows','c','Correct.',true],['Give them water','c','Water can make it worse.',false]]},c:{text:'You switch to 5 abdominal thrusts and keep switching until the food comes out. Then you tell them to see a doctor.',end:true}})]],
["Choking in special situations",["If the person is **pregnant** or **very large**, you cannot reach around the belly well. Give **chest thrusts** instead: put your fist on the **center of the breastbone** and push straight back.","For a **baby** under 1 year, never give belly pushes. Hold the baby face down along your arm with the head lower than the chest. Give **5 back blows** between the shoulder blades. Then turn the baby face up and give **5 chest thrusts** with two fingers just below the nipple line. Repeat.","If you are **alone and choking**, call 911 if you can. Push your fist into your own belly, just above the belly button, or lean hard over the back of a chair. If a choking person **stops responding**, lower them to the floor, call 911 and start CPR.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Who is choking and what to do","pairs":[["Adult or child","5 back blows and 5 belly thrusts"],["Pregnant person","Chest thrusts"],["Very large person","Chest thrusts"],["Baby under 1","5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts"],["You, alone","Push your belly against a chair"],["Person stops responding","Lower to the floor and start CPR"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Mild choking: the person can cough, talk or breathe—let them cough; severe choking: cannot talk, cough or breathe, hands on throat—say the signs out loud and help now.','Ask “Are you choking? Can I help you?”; stand to the side and a little behind, arm across the chest, bend forward, 5 back blows with the heel of the hand between the shoulder blades.','Then fist thumb side in, just above the belly button and below the breastbone, 5 abdominal thrusts in and up; switch 5 and 5 until it comes out; tell the person to see a doctor; use hand sanitizer.'],terms:[['Severe choking','A blocked airway—the person cannot talk, cough or breathe.'],['Back blows','Firm hits between the shoulder blades to loosen an object.'],['Abdominal thrusts','Quick in-and-up pushes on the belly to push an object out.']],mcqs:[q('Which sign means severe choking?','The person cannot talk or cough','The person is coughing hard','The person is talking','The person is laughing','Help right away.'),q('What should you do for mild choking?','Let them keep coughing','Give abdominal thrusts','Give water','Leave','Coughing helps.'),q('Where do back blows go?','Between the shoulder blades','On the lower back','On the head','On the arm','Heel of the hand.'),q('How many back blows do you give before switching?','5','1','10','20','Then 5 thrusts.'),q('Where does your fist go for abdominal thrusts?','Just above the belly button','On the breastbone','On the neck','Below the belly button','Thumb side in.'),q('Which way do you push?','In and up','Down','Side to side','Out only','Quick thrusts.'),q('What do you tell the person after the object comes out?','See a doctor right away','Run a lap','Eat faster','Nothing','Check for injury.'),q('How long is Skill VI?','3 minutes','10 minutes','30 seconds','8 minutes','Short skill.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to help a person who is choking badly, following HOSA Skill VI.',rubric:[['Recognize and ask',['cannot talk','cough','breathe','throat','say the signs','are you choking','can i help','nod']],['Back blows',['side','behind','arm','chest','bend forward','heel','5','shoulder blades']],['Abdominal thrusts',['behind','waist','fist','thumb','above the belly button','below the breastbone','in and up','5']],['After',['switch','until','object','out','doctor','sanitizer']]],model:'First I look for signs of severe choking: the person cannot talk, cough or breathe and is holding their throat. I say these signs out loud for the judge. I ask, “Are you choking? Can I help you?” and the person nods yes. I stand to the side and a little behind them, put one arm across their chest for support and bend them forward at the waist until their upper body is almost flat. With the heel of my other hand, I give 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades, each one a separate try to knock the object loose. Next, I stand behind them and wrap my arms around their waist. I make a fist and put the thumb side in the middle of the belly, just above the belly button and well below the breastbone, and I hold my fist with my other hand. I give 5 quick abdominal thrusts, pushing in and up. I keep switching between 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until the object comes out. After it comes out, I tell the person to see a doctor right away, because the thrusts can hurt the inside of the body. I talk kindly the whole time and finish by using hand sanitizer. If the person stops responding, I would help them to the ground, call 911 and start CPR.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_CHOKE,AHA_CPR]};
